Now, on something of a tangent to this: I've read up on the famous Zadir 
run, which netted the legopowa, the player, exactly 1 point shy of 
NetHack's score counter turn-over (2^31 - 1 points); that score is 
permanently the top score on the NAO high-score list.  legopowa did not 
save-scum to succeed, but he/she used macros and had help from paxed (a 
nethack.alt.org administrator) in recovering from a series of crashes 
that would have ordinarily ended the run prematurely.

What I gather from this is that the "cheating" line is drawn firmly 
based on knowledge - so long as something could, in theory, be done or 
learned by one person with a sufficient stock of time and patience, it 
is not considered cheating; what legopowa did in a days with macros 
could be done in years by an insanely dedicated player repeating those 
macros "by hand".  Of course, the fact that nobody can actually improve 
on legopowa's score is due to an imbalance in the game, and as anybody 
into hard-core Street Fighter would tell you, it's a perfectly 
legitimate strategy (often the only viable one, in competition-level 
play) to exploit game imbalances left in by the game's designers.

Were games like the Zadir game the reason that NAO recently implemented 
the Z-scores ranking system alongside the traditional high-scores 
ranking?  There's no macro for ascending, obviously :).


